# Files `iterion fmt` refuses, and why the check is green anyway.
#
# The list is EMPTY: #1612 gave the writer its multi-line forms — the `|`
# block scalar at a property line, the backtick raw string elsewhere — and
# every file that stood here is formatted now. What remains refusable is a
# file holding a value no form carries over lines (a newline beside a
# backtick or a carriage return that the block scalar cannot hold), which
# one folded value re-renders without either form, exactly as before.
#
# This list is a RATCHET, not a permission slip. `task fmt:check` is green
# while the refusals are exactly these. It goes red, naming the file, when:
#   - a formattable file drifts out of its canonical form;
#   - a new file becomes unformattable (adding one here needs a reason);
#   - a file listed here stops being refused — including one renamed or
#     deleted.
#
# Format the file and delete its line. Do not add one without a ticket.
#
# The list holds PATHS, not reasons: a file named here that later fails to
# parse, or whose comments a rewrite would lose, is still merely "refused"
# and the check stays green on it. The floor under that is elsewhere —
# bots/catalog_parse_compile_test.go, inside the required `test` job, which
# parses and compiles every catalogue bot.
#
# https://github.com/SocialGouv/iterion/issues/1612
